  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "Sj39UdQvvsMXtU0ho4wYpb5YjEs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:45:16.505Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`LICENSE.md` states: *\"You may use or modify the software only for your own internal business purposes or for non-commercial or personal use. You may distribute the software or provide it to others only if you do so free of charge for non-commercial purposes.\"* Source files containing `.ee.` are separately excluded and require a commercial Enterprise licence.\n\nGitHub's licence detector returns `NOASSERTION` \u2014 not a recognised SPDX or OSI licence. n8n is consistent and honest about this, describing itself as *\"fair-code\"* and *\"source available\"*, never as open source; faircode.io states plainly that *\"Fair-code is not a software license.\"*\n\nThe consequence is procedural, not moral. Any EU or German tender that requires OSI-approved open source cannot count n8n, regardless of the fact that the source is readable and self-hosting is permitted. A second and sharper question \u2014 whether a public body running n8n as a cost-recharged shared service for municipalities collides with the prohibition on \"hosting n8n and charging people money to access it\" \u2014 needs legal advice rather than a technical reading.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-20T08:45:16.505042Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:45:16.505047Z"
    }
  },
